      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "821c7b8f-f0f3-59a5-941d-b60d4d0303e5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: George Armistead, Edward Berge\n- Places: Fort McHenry, Baltimore, Maryland, Baltimore Harbor, Chesapeake Bay\n- Events: Battle of Baltimore, War of 1812, World War I, World War II\n- Monuments: Armistead Monument\n\nFacts:\n- Fort McHenry was first built in 1798 in Baltimore, Maryland.\n- George Armistead was born on April 10, 1780, and he later became an American military officer.\n- During the War of 1812, George Armistead served as the commander of Fort McHenry during the Battle of Baltimore.\n- On September 13–14, 1814, Fort McHenry successfully defended Baltimore Harbor from an attack by the British navy from the Chesapeake Bay.\n- Fort McHenry is a historical American coastal pentagonal bastion fort best known for its role in the War of 1812.\n- George Armistead died on April 25, 1818.\n- Fort McHenry was used continuously by U.S. armed forces through World War I and by the Coast Guard in World War II.\n- The Armistead Monument is a bronze statue of Col. George Armistead created by Edward Berge.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a6b423a9-631f-5e06-9e80-1d265a03e6e8",
